      "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
