    "q-325860": {
      "dom_id": "step-q325860",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9} & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14193836/assets/5a1377fa0ff46ff7a7e4b685d38852ad67abd8c0adfc432fc22d7c1d57b7a57f.png)",
