    "q-16645": {
      "dom_id": "step-q16645",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
