    "q-326043": {
      "dom_id": "step-q326043",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}+18x+32\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+18x+9 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[{x}^{2}+2x]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+1)^{2}-1]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}-9+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
