    "q-326285": {
      "dom_id": "step-q326285",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{3})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{18}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ 72\\,(\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{8}) & =72 \\\\ 16+9(y-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ 9(y-3)^{2} & =56 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =\\frac{56}{9} \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{9}}=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{3}).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
