      "source_example_id": "e-5220",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse",
      "kp_id": "e-5220"
    },
    "q-16682": {
      "dom_id": "step-q16682",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=-\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14193836/assets/4ddd101282e0a45d9e5fb16ed75c922c10f859cf13fe5e35a892994879e2b4ff.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
