    "q-16684": {
      "dom_id": "step-q16684",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14193836/assets/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)",
