    "q-9468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q9468",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}+4y]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y+2)^{2}-4]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}-24+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $6(y+2)^{2}=-4.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
