    "q-7646": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7646",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y=10$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =10 \\\\ (0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4(0)+4y & =10 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+4y & =10 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =5+1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,-1+\\sqrt{6}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-7646-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{3})",
