    "q-7649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q7649",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6x-4y=5$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6x-4y & =5 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}+6(0)-4y & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =5+4 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$ and $(0,5).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
